## Ephemeral execution workers

A release-installed controller launches local work through the existing private
worker transport. Startup and the existing update scheduler prepare verified
execution artifacts. At the launch selection boundary, the latest prepared
compatible artifact and the effective workflow and policy revision are captured. The policy must match the existing claim; a mismatch refuses this launch
through instance failure handling so a retry can acquire the current policy.
This is a recorded local selection, not a transaction spanning release and policy
publication. Releases prepared after selection affect the next attempt. A retry
selects again; inline phases of the same attempt retain and verify its original
artifact and workflow, including after newer releases or policies appear.

Checksums, signatures, release provenance, binary identity and the host platform
are verified before a child starts. Verified archives are reused locally, but
release discovery belongs to startup and the existing update lifecycle. Attempts
verify the selected binary digest and protocol without querying release metadata.
A discovery outage preserves the prepared selection; missing prepared artifacts
and explicit critical or incompatible updates refuse launch. Verification failures
never install an unverified candidate. Active workers retain their artifacts.
Retaining an artifact for an existing attempt checks its captured binary digest
without selecting a different release.
Corrupt cached archives are evicted and fetched through the same signature and
checksum verification path. Archive ownership follows the associated execution
artifact; obsolete archives are removed after their final owner exits.

Ordinary approved policy changes flow through project reconciliation or managed
policy application without pausing the project or waiting for active attempts.
Only new claims and launches use the updated workflow; captured continuations
keep their original workflow. Isolation-changing local binding operations still
use their existing settled-work contract. Refused reloads record their complete
error chain through the existing workflow reload diagnostics.

The existing private worker protocol is version 5. It carries the captured claim
isolation policy explicitly across the worker process boundary, alongside typed
session process registration and cleanup scoped to one channel. Controllers and
workers must both support this version; older peers cannot silently discard the
claim policy and substitute backend defaults. A controller can launch
any newer verified execution release advertising this protocol on its platform;
its own build number is not a compatibility ceiling. Children never open the
controller's SQLite store, run migrations, claim work, renew leases or publish a
competing completion. The controller performs these operations and all Hub
communication under the captured lease and fence. Authentication, grants,
revocations and emergency controls remain live even when ordinary policy is
captured. Local provider process records stay in the controller's existing
journal and reaper; remote SSH process IDs remain remote.

On Linux and macOS the execution child has a dedicated process group and owned
output pipes. The controller observes parent exit independently of inherited
output descriptors. It keeps the channel, immutable artifact and any managed Go
cache consumer lock through finalization and cleanup. Paid provider descendants
use the existing journaled process identities, process-group termination and
scratch cleanup. A provider result is not completion: failed outcome delivery
retains the existing completion owner and capacity for its normal retry. Cleanup
must finish before the claim owner releases capacity. Old artifacts are pruned
only when their final owner releases them; the selected latest artifact remains a
cache. Compatible versions share this typed protocol and the existing managed
cache admission/consumer file locks, rather than a shared SQLite schema.

Routine automatic, explicit and Hub-requested updates prepare verified execution
artifacts without draining. An idle reservation may install and restart the
controller through the existing supervisor; active attempts keep their processes.
The controller otherwise remains on its captured build while newer compatible
workers use free slots. The existing exceptional drain replaces the controller
only for release metadata identifying a critical security update or a verified
worker reporting an incompatible protocol. Its log records the reason and runner
scope. Intentional operator maintenance drains retain their existing owner;
urgent requests, following the Hub and an elapsed deferral period are not
compatibility requirements.

Existing update receipts distinguish the running controller from an applied
execution artifact. An applied receipt with `execution_artifact: true` settles
preparation without claiming the controller restarted; newer ordinary and
follow-Hub requests can replace it while active work retains the older controller.
Enrolled update discovery refreshes on its existing check interval so subsequent
ordinary requests can select newer available releases.
Existing attempt runtime reads expose `worker_launch` with
the selected execution version, commit, digest, protocol, captured policy ID and
revision, selection time and observed process identity. These are provenance in
the existing reads, not another dashboard surface or state owner.

## Concurrent migrations and generated files

New Goose SQL migrations in `internal/store/migrations` and
`internal/hubserver/migrations` use UTC timestamp versions:
`YYYYMMDDHHMMSS_name.sql`. Use a descriptive name and Goose's default timestamp
mode, never `-s` (sequential mode):

```sh
make db-create NAME=add_feature
make db-create NAME=add_feature MIGRATIONS_DIR=internal/hubserver/migrations
```

The target runs `TZ=UTC goose -dir "$MIGRATIONS_DIR" create "$NAME" sql`.
The equivalent direct command is
`TZ=UTC goose -dir internal/store/migrations create add_feature sql`.
Existing numbered files remain unchanged: store through 68 and Hub through 71.
The older descriptive-name boundaries (store 68, Hub 70, registry 5, auth 4)
remain fixed in the checker; registry and auth retain their sequential convention.
`make check-migrations` diagnoses duplicate versions and invalid timestamp names
in the existing scheduled suite or during optional focused diagnostics.

Adding a SQL migration changes only its new file. The embedded directory supplies
the migration set and supported Hub version; the historical Go data conversions
remain registered at their original versions. Timestamp versions sort after
every historical sequential version. Fresh databases apply them in version
order. Existing databases apply pending files through Goose, including older
timestamps that land after a newer timestamp, without renumbering at landing.
Hub foreign-key verification stays in the last pending migration's transaction,
including when that migration has an older timestamp than the database maximum.

Independent migrations with distinct timestamps merge in either order. Timestamps
have one-second precision, so simultaneous creation can still produce a duplicate
version; the checker reports it even when descriptive filenames differ. Choose a
distinct UTC timestamp before publishing an unapplied migration. Never rename or
renumber a migration that may already have been applied; use a forward repair for
deployed collisions. Migrations that depend on another branch's schema still
require their source dependency to land first and an appropriate version order.

sqlc reads `internal/store/migrations` through `sqlc/sqlc.yaml`, including timestamp
files. Run `make sqlc` after schema or query changes, or `make generate` when
other generated inputs also changed. `make db-migrate` applies store migrations
with Goose's `-allow-missing` option; Hub migrations run automatically at startup.

sqlc and Templ Go output retain the Git merge attribute unset. Merge their
source inputs, then regenerate and stage only that Go output with `make generate`;
choosing one branch's output does not resolve the combined source. Ordinary
source files retain Git's text merge behavior.

This source convention does not add a required check, strict branch freshness,
merge worker, or recovery path. It exposes these collisions to existing Git
conflict handling; unrelated semantic conflicts still rely on review and the
scheduled suite.

## Generated artifact inventory

| Artifact | Source | Owner |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `docs/config.md` and `config.reference.yaml` | Authored `docs/config.md.in`, typed config, defaults and validators | `make generate-docs`, tests and docs publish; ignored |
| `static/css/output.css` | `static/css/input.css`, templates and npm lockfile | `make assets` at build time; ignored |
| `static/app/conversation/**` | `web/conversation` source and npm lockfile | `make assets` at build time; ignored |
| `internal/store/sqlc/*.go` | SQL queries, migrations and pinned sqlc config | Committed: plain `go build` needs these types and query methods |
| `internal/web/**/*_templ.go` | Templ sources and pinned Templ version | Committed: plain `go build` needs the generated render functions |

Go does not run generators during compilation. The two generated-Go exceptions
allow a plain checkout to build and test with Go alone. All other generated
outputs are ignored. `make build` prepares assets and docs without regenerating
tracked Go; `make generate` explicitly regenerates Go after source changes.
Vendored JavaScript/fonts, authored OpenAPI, SQL migrations and reviewed test
fixtures are inputs, not outputs of these generators.

`make test` and `make test-fast` generate docs before testing.
`make check-generated` generates config docs and retains `sqlc diff` only for
committed SQL output. The scheduled suite runs this target on its pinned commit, so
generator failures still fail scheduled validation. It does not compare ignored
output with Git. Optional `configdoc -check` compares existing local output with
fresh rendering; plain `go test` does not require generated docs.

## Visual tests

Playwright layout tests live under `tests/visual/` and run against isolated
`detent dev-runtime` instances on port `0`. Install Chromium once, then run:

```sh
npx playwright install chromium
make visual-e2e
```

Every host runs the same browser journeys and outcome assertions. UI changes
attach screenshots (light/dark, desktop/390 as relevant) to the issue or PR as
review evidence for human or AI comparison against the approved design.
Screenshots are never compared by pixels or hashes in any test or gate.

Do not commit `tmp/playwright-evidence`, `tmp/playwright-report`, or
`tmp/playwright-results`.

## Published documentation

The detent.build `cmd/docs-sync` consumer must materialize its verified pinned
upstream commit as a full source tree, then run `make generate-docs` in that
tree before selecting published files. This needs Go 1.26, not Node or the
sqlc/Templ executables. Keep its tag-object and peeled-commit verification.
Copy authored docs from that same tree and include generated `docs/config.md`.
Exclude the input `docs/config.md.in` from the published inventory. Hash generated content for the existing manifest;
there is no Git blob OID for ignored outputs. Preserve the source commit/tag
provenance and existing publication transaction. A Git-only `ls-tree` or
`git show` docs sync cannot publish the generated pages.

Do not advance the site's pinned upstream release to this layout until that
companion source update is available. Site publication remains separate from
Detent's build and scheduled validation.

## Hub analytics rollups

Diagnostics, Reports and `analytics_dashboard` read the persisted
`analytics_hourly_rollups` model through the WAL reader pool. Ready decisions,
skip reasons, merge entries and lease capacity have additive hourly values.
Residence reads subtract accumulated per-issue lane and queue durations from
hourly snapshots. Indexed state-boundary samples supply exact partial-hour
edges, Todo depths and population clipping. Open spans contribute elapsed time
from their recorded start without a timer or event replay. Completion samples
retain the accumulated durations at Done for completion quantiles and throughput.
Claim waits have additive hourly durations; indexed claim and skip samples retain
bounded population cursors. The same model retains current issue, attempt and
usage facts for exact quantiles and distinct populations. It excludes observation-event
payload history. Landing and refusal identities remain bounded drill-downs.

The existing native commit path projects changed work items before committing.
Triggers on events, issues, attempts, leases and usage mark items in
`analytics_rollup_dirty`; project, policy and workflow-state changes do not.
A commit projects at most `analyticsBackfillBatch` dirty items from their
per-item event sequence positions; the rest stay marked for the next commit or
the existing maintenance tick. Every projection statement is scoped by
organization and project and looks up the work item through an index;
`TestAnalyticsCommitPlans` fails on a full scan or on an index lookup bounded
only by organization or project. Residence maintenance resumes from the last stored per-issue
sequence and processes only new transitions and claims. Event rows hold the
event identity, time and the decision and transition fields the counters use;
payloads stay in `collaboration_events` and are joined by id. Unchanged lease
snapshots do not rewrite capacity buckets. Lease capacity buckets extend at most
24 hours past the projecting commit and a renewal reprojects them, so a lease
that is neither renewed nor released for more than 24 hours stops adding
capacity until its next write.

Each work item projects inside its own savepoint: analytics failure does not
roll back an event, claim or lane transition, or another item's projection. A
failed item keeps its last sequence position and is marked failed. It is
retried on its next change and hourly on the existing maintenance tick, which
rotates through failed items in batches, not on every commit. While a project has a failed item, its reads use the raw
computation.

The migration only creates the schema. Existing projects are listed for
backfill, and the existing maintenance tick backfills them after startup in
batches of at most `analyticsBackfillBatch` work items and
`analyticsBackfillEvents` events, each in its own short write transaction, through the same projector and positions. Until a project's
backfill completes, its reads use the raw computation, and commits skip its
unprojected items.

`rebuildAnalyticsRollups` rebuilds one organization/project in a caller-owned
transaction from the raw sources, including the hourly counters, residence
snapshots, completion and decision samples, and lease buckets. It replaces the
project's rollups and sequence positions atomically and is the test oracle.
A migration that changes a metric definition deletes the affected projects'
rollups and positions and lists those projects for backfill again.

Attempt projections decode an explicit set of report fields. Completion text,
handoffs, worker metadata, command output and artifact bodies stay in their
source records; adding a runtime field does not add it to analytics.

The existing retention pass keeps exact capacity, skip, residence and lease
samples for 30 days, rounded outward to an hour. Older samples become typed
hourly counts, sums, extrema and coverage in `analytics_sample_hours`. Skip
samples use the existing hourly skip totals. Residence retains the last
per-issue snapshot before its compacted boundary so open spans and later
transitions continue correctly. A project slot cutoff prevents lease refreshes
from recreating archived capacity. Compaction is bounded to 5,000 short
transactions and the existing backfill time budget per pass.

Older residence duration distributions describe issue-hours rather than whole
issues. Counts therefore count contributing issue-hours; duration sums and
transition/cause counts remain additive. Quantiles merge histogram populations
with 16 logarithmic bins per octave using `log2(seconds + 1)`, interpolating
within each bin's observed extrema; they never average percentiles. Windows
cutting an archived hour prorate its duration sum and report partial coverage.
Coverage strings name the older hourly granularity. Project-wide archived
aggregates cannot satisfy issue-level filters; filtered reports mark that
historical portion unavailable while retaining their exact recent results.
Cost, cached tokens and
usage totals retain their existing exact hourly facts.

Scheduler claim samples capture their exact predecessor before raw observation
rows can be compacted. A rebuild cannot recreate deleted predecessor evidence:
a sequence gap makes that capacity-wait interval unavailable. It must not infer
adjacency from the next surviving earlier event. Current runtime observations
and usage remain rebuildable from their durable source records.

Hosted audit retains security, administration, mutation, support-access and
refusal/error records for 30 days. Routine successful reads and catalog calls
produce no audit row, including reads transported through POST key calls;
those calls are classified by their resolved operation. The retention pass
also removes legacy successful GET envelopes in batches of at most 5,000,
excluding errors, refusals, security events and support access. Account
administration receipts keep their existing retry semantics.

## Conversation assets

Conversation source and its npm lockfile are tracked; `static/app/conversation/`
is ignored build output. Do not stage that directory in feature commits.
`make app` installs the locked dependencies and builds the entire client.
`make assets` also prepares the dashboard stylesheet.
`make generate` runs it before Go generators, and `make build` embeds that
output. Use Node 24 for the same toolchain as staging and release builds.

For a private operator build, first select the complete source composition in
this isolated checkout, then run `make build`. When supplying custom Go flags
or cross-compiling, run `make assets` followed by the desired `go build` command.
`make assets` also generates `static/release-notes.json` from local Git history.
Production builds supply the same `VERSION`, `COMMIT`, and `DATE` to this target
and the binary linker flags, including operator version strings. Generation
requires a full checkout with release tags and makes no network requests. The
ignored feed is embedded with the static inputs and included in release source
archives; it is never committed.
Always regenerate after changing or integrating client source or the lockfile;
a direct Go build cannot determine whether existing ignored output is stale.
A plain checkout compiles without Node or generated assets. Its embedded tree
contains the tracked static inputs; prepare assets before running the UI.
The existing missing-client response applies to an unprepared binary.
Prepared release source archives already contain the embed inputs and
`BUILD_LDFLAGS`, so end users compile those with Go alone.

Staging and scheduled fresh-checkout consumers call the repository's
`prepare-conversation` action, which uses this same `make assets` entry point.
GoReleaser's before hook prepares assets and docs for release and snapshot
builds and includes all output in its prepared source archive. Every consumer
builds from its pinned checkout; assets from a different feature branch are never reused.

`make check-app` retains typechecking, client unit tests, rebuilding and
attribution diagnostics. It does not compare against committed bundles and is
not a shipping gate. The scheduled suite validates integrated source as before.

To reproduce the contention regression and exercise embedded delivery, run:

```sh
python3 scripts/verify-conversation-build.py
```

The diagnostic creates a repository under the provided worker scratch directory,
merges two independent client edits with source-only commits, builds a local
GoReleaser snapshot, and compiles its prepared source with Go alone. Both builds
exercise the existing embedded HTTP handler for every conversation asset from
an arbitrary working directory. It neither publishes a release nor touches
tracker state, shared refs or the running service.
